.stage/perf-logic-finish-merged-pipeline/raw/issue1748-current.json:168:      "body": "Extended the source-emission work to typed owners in the existing correspondence worktree (Principles 4, 7, 9 and 17\u201319).\n\n- Rule, reasoning-contract/preset, path-shape, constraint/sugar, reasoning-program, correspondence and referenced transaction-leg reads now capture their actual native source root at invocation. Successful values retain source and diagnostic references through canonical sorting; their final IR positions are assigned while detaching the paired values. Original diagnostics remain attached even when a structurally representable contract has an explicit native-admission error.\n- Rejected owners retain their own diagnostic indices. A constraint failing because of an already-reported malformed formula references that original diagnostic instead of losing the owner or issuing a duplicate warning. Named-graph owner declarations have explicit default-graph exclusions.\n- The correspondence reader no longer builds an owned `(String, String) -> Vec<RdfTerm>` copy of the dataset. Its borrowed `CorrespondenceView` uses PurRDF's existing indexes and the same native default-graph boundary as the compiler, including native annotations and duplicate physical representations. Leg reads construct only this borrowed view, and shared leg references are read once.\n- Correspondence fields in another graph cannot supply or override selected default-graph fields. Anonymous correspondence source identity is explicitly `UNLOWERED_CORRESPONDENCE_IDENTITY`: it is a current named-IR lowering limitation, not a newly invented prohibition in the normative calculus. Referenced legs without a supported selected path program retain `UNLOWERED_TRANSACTION_PROGRAM` evidence.\n- Existing cross-correspondence recovery-identity and constraint/program uniqueness checks remain in the shared IR builders. Internal ordered entry points let the traced compiler reuse those checks without sorting the values again.\n- `compile-logic.v17-native-owner-emissions` invalidates the prior producer action; `docs/PIPELINE_SPINE.md` describes the actual evidence and native read boundaries.\n\nValidation passes: `make fmt`; workspace Clippy (`--lib --tests --examples`, `-D warnings`, 19.48s); `make lint`; and **653/653** selected compiler frontend/IR/restriction/projection tests, including six new GMEOW owner/native-read regressions. Final nextest run `5793dc14-c154-4bf5-bc14-27bdae0b269c`; compile 2m39s, execution 0.218s. An earlier 653/653 run also passed before the anonymous-identity diagnostic refinement. No corpus generation, upstream conformance suite, full gate or producer performance run occurred; no snapshot changes were needed. The initial Clippy pass caught and fixed an internal visibility mismatch and the removed index's unused import. Existing Cargo manifest/future-compatibility warnings remain plan work.\n\nThis is root-level extraction evidence, not completed all-family coverage, native execution or a rewrite certificate. Current implementation still must account for nested owner/component consumption, every restriction/list statement, UniqueLang and native schema/existential producers, required-but-undeclared units and selected obligations. Complete `CompiledTheory` selection and certified program-aware execution/admission remain required. Anonymous native identity lowering and strict correspondence-field admission also remain required: the borrowed reader still inherits scalar first-match/optional-enum/decimal and some multivalue filtering behavior that must not silently erase malformed authored metadata. Preserve all existing calculus, fusion, target/worked-example, production, zero-upstream-overlap, O3/full-LTO measurement and independent-cold-CI requirements. No issue, full gate or merge is claimed complete; changes are uncommitted.\n",
