.stage/perf-logic-finish-merged-pipeline/raw/issue1748-current.json:333:      "body": "Native rule admission and session identity integration in the preserved correspondence worktree (Principles 4, 7, 9, 17-19):\n\nThe direct native rule lowerer could silently discard an aggregate, turn a negative conclusion positive, or execute a non-object-level rule as an ordinary derivation. It now rejects those unsupported operators with their source identity before preparation, execution or certification. This prevents an aggregate declaration from being certified as an ordinary join; implementing the aggregate/numeric capability required by #1742 remains current scope.\n\nSource-context admission now covers direct callers and cached materialization, including annotated execution and each PositiveHorn, StratifiedNaf, WellFounded and StableModel selection. Reusable preparation retains source scope without granting execution. Public materialization admits that retained obligation before loading a world or visiting a demand source; hydrated operator rules use the same admission. Means-end sessions consume the producer-owned program and preparation together, removing the detached rule-slice entry point. Tests use derivable input, verify ordinary execution, then prove scoped variants are rejected without evaluating annotation callbacks.\n\nSession classification now preserves the actual source-rule error instead of repeating full-native preparation and relabeling the refusal as floundering. Both paths use the same source-rule lowering, so that second probe supplied no additional capability. Other admitted non-incremental fragments retain their existing full-native classification. The engine descriptor now also binds the operator preparation, means-end execution and complete session protocol implementation (classification, identity, delta, journal, checkpoint and restore). Previous checkpoints therefore cannot attest this execution contract.\n\nValidation: 113 behavioral tests passed, including all native session integration suites, cached source admission across all four profiles, source evidence retention, materialization and shared preparation. The two intentional descriptor pin changes were then verified by 3/3 focused pin/inventory checks. The behavioral run took 0.338 s after a 3m05s workspace-inventory rebuild; these are development test observations, not pipeline performance measurements. Workspace Clippy with Rust warnings denied passed in 21.08 s; repository `make lint` passed. No corpus was produced by a test, and no PurRDF conformance suite ran.\n\nThis closes specific silent-execution paths; it does not complete selected-source coverage, contextual/sorted/modal/deontic execution, canonical composition axes, the full correspondence calculus, fusion, role projection or production adoption. Those remain implementation requirements here and under #1704/#1741/#1742/#1745. Production optimization still blocks #1738. Fresh O3/full-LTO producer observations, authenticated corpus consumers, both independent cold CI generations, the full required gate, review and ghprsq merge remain required. No full gate, commit, push or merge is claimed for this batch.\n",
